<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dositej Obradović</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1739–<a href="/years/1811">1811</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dositej Obradović was a Serbian writer, translator and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> thinker who promoted writing in the vernacular Serbian language rather than Church Slavonic. After decades of travel and study across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-serbian-uprising">First Serbian Uprising</a> and became the first minister of education of Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, helping to found the Great School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1808.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dositej-obradovic.jpg" alt="Dositej Obradović" width="308" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dositej_obradovic_Novi_Sad.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dositej Obradović was born Dimitrije Obradović, probably in 1739, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banat">Banat</a> village of Čakovo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, now Ciacova in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. He grew up bilingual in Serbian and Romanian and learned classical Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, modern Greek, German, English, French, Russian and Italian. On 17 February 1757 he became a monk in the Serbian Orthodox monastery of Hopovo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a> and took the name Dositej. He translated many European classics into Serbian, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aesop">Aesop</a>'s Fables, and on 2 November 1760 left the monastery for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilandar">Hilandar</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1761 he studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> and then taught in Serbian schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, among them Kninsko Polje, Golubić near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knin">Knin</a> and Plavno. He was ordained a priest by Vasilije Petrović. He studied Greek on Corfu and in 1782 enrolled at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-halle">University of Halle</a> to study philosophy. In 1783 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, where he published his first work and studied under Johann Eberhard, a disciple of Christian Wolf. In 1785 he presented his Leipzig books to the British Museum Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the first modern Serbian books it acquired.</p><p>More than a third of his life was spent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, where he was influenced by Joseph II and the German Enlightenment. He was also an Anglophile who saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as the land of spiritual freedom, and his forty years of travel took him across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-serbian-uprising">First Serbian Uprising</a> of 1804 he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where he published the poem Vostani Serbije in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karadorde">Karađorđe Petrović</a> and the insurgents. In 1807 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> at Karađorđe's invitation and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>'s first minister of education.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> he founded the Velika škola, the first higher education institution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, which later developed into a university. He helped introduce the literature of western European countries to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and, with Vuk Karadžić, whom he influenced, is recognised as a father of modern Serbian literature. Because the Serbian population often suffered famine, he also introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> cultivation. He died in Belgrade in 1811 and was buried in the Cathedral of Saint Archangel Michael after a large funeral procession.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dositej Obradović” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The scholar <b>Dositej Obradović</b> became the first minister of education, and a Great School opened in Belgrade in 1808.</blockquote><a href="/state/revolutionary-serbia">Revolutionary Serbia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ciacova, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.5,21.13&amp;year=1785" aria-label="Ciacova on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5051 559 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dositej Obradović"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5501 829h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5501 829h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.5,21.13&amp;year=1785">What was here in 1785? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
